      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "location_description"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Federal farmhouse Spring Grove was built in 1834 for Robert Murphy, a post‑Revolutionary newcomer to the Northern Neck.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.dhr.virginia.gov/historic-registers/096-0023/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Spring Grove’s design showcases a dwarf Ionic portico and interior woodwork inspired by Asher Benjamin’s pattern books.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17,
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.dhr.virginia.gov/historic-registers/096-0023/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The house remains largely unchanged and overlooks Nomini Creek from a high, flat ridge in Westmoreland County.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.dhr.virginia.gov/historic-registers/096-0023/",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Spring Grove sits at the crossroads of Virginia State Routes 10 and 40, just under eight miles west‑northwest of Surry.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spring_Grove%2C_Virginia",
